# Caddy — friendly hostnames instead of ports.
#
# Caddy reaches each service over the Compose network by service name and
# *container* port. That is deliberately not the same as the published host
# port: Sonarr publishes 127.0.0.1:8989 on the host, and Caddy talks to
# sonarr:8989 inside the network.
#
# WHAT IS PROXIED, AND WHAT IS NOT
#
# Only household services are proxied. Every admin service — the *arrs, SABnzbd,
# qBittorrent, Prowlarr, NZBHydra2, Bindery — is bound to loopback on purpose,
# and putting a hostname in front of one publishes a full-control surface, most
# with weak or disabled default authentication, to every device on the network.
# Stanzas for them are at the bottom, commented out, with what you are accepting
# written next to them.
#
# Set DOMAIN in .env. With a real domain whose DNS points here, Caddy obtains
# genuine certificates automatically. For a name no public authority issues a
# certificate for, this proxy serves plain HTTP rather than a certificate a
# browser warns about. Jellyfin's own TLS is the door's: it serves 8920 with a
# certificate lemonfiber issues from the stack's own root, which a client pins
# by its fingerprint (config/door/Caddyfile).
#
# The default below keeps this file valid before DOMAIN is set. A `.local` name
# cannot be issued a public certificate, so Caddy serves plain HTTP until you
# point a real domain here.
#
# It listens on 8080 and 8443, which Compose publishes as 80 and 443: a port
# below 1024 needs a kernel capability no service here holds. To receive
# certificate-expiry notices from the CA, add `email you@example.com` to the
# block below.

{
	http_port 8080
	https_port 8443
}

# ── household ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Jellyfin is reached through the door, which guards every byte it serves and
# trusts this proxy's fixed address alone to say which client a request came
# from. Caddy names the client in X-Forwarded-For and ignores one a client sends.
watch.{$DOMAIN:home.local} {
	reverse_proxy door:8096
}

requests.{$DOMAIN:home.local} {
	reverse_proxy seerr:5055
}

books.{$DOMAIN:home.local} {
	reverse_proxy calibre-web-automated:8083
}

listen.{$DOMAIN:home.local} {
	reverse_proxy audiobookshelf:80
}

home.{$DOMAIN:home.local} {
	reverse_proxy homepage:3000
}

# ── admin — deliberately disabled ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Uncommenting any of these publishes an admin surface to the LAN. That is
# permitted, but it is a decision: the service's own authentication becomes the
# only thing between your network and full control of the stack. Put a Caddy
# `basic_auth` or forward-auth in front of it, and record why.
#
# sonarr.{$DOMAIN:home.local}      { reverse_proxy sonarr:8989 }
# radarr.{$DOMAIN:home.local}      { reverse_proxy radarr:7878 }
# lidarr.{$DOMAIN:home.local}      { reverse_proxy lidarr:8686 }
# bindery.{$DOMAIN:home.local}     { reverse_proxy bindery:8787 }
# bazarr.{$DOMAIN:home.local}      { reverse_proxy bazarr:6767 }
# prowlarr.{$DOMAIN:home.local}    { reverse_proxy prowlarr:9696 }
# nzbhydra.{$DOMAIN:home.local}    { reverse_proxy nzbhydra2:5076 }
# sabnzbd.{$DOMAIN:home.local}     { reverse_proxy sabnzbd:8080 }
# qbittorrent.{$DOMAIN:home.local} { reverse_proxy gluetun:8081 }
